\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs42\fsmilli21333 \cf2  \cf2 PART XVI \'97 Production Agentic Engineering\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 33 \'97 Agent Guardrails\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Input validation, prompt injection and indirect prompt injection.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Excessive agency, sensitive-data leakage, tool misuse and output validation.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Human approval and least privilege.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Add deterministic controls around the agent and every sensitive tool.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Test malicious inputs and unauthorized tool requests.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Prompt instructions vs enforceable controls; injection defense; least privilege; approval gates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 34 \'97 Structured Agent Output\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Typed decisions, JSON schema, Jackson deserialization and Bean Validation.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Malformed output handling, controlled retry and failure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Create AgentDecision and ToolRequest records.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Validate every model-generated software-facing decision before execution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Why arbitrary prose is unsafe for machine decisions; schema validation and retry strategy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 35 \'97 Human-in-the-Loop\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Risk classification and approval gates.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Low-risk automatic execution vs high-risk human review.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Require approval before sending email, modifying customer data, issuing refund or deleting information.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Persist proposed action, reviewer decision and audit evidence.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Where humans belong in agent workflows; approval state; auditability.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs42\fsmilli21333 \cf2 PART XVII \'97 AI Evaluation\

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 36 \'97 Agent Evaluation\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Evaluation datasets containing input, expected tool/evidence/behavior and forbidden behavior.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Task success, tool accuracy, groundedness, retrieval quality, schema compliance, hallucination, safety, latency, tokens and cost.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Create a regression evaluation suite.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Run it before prompt/model/tool changes and compare to an accepted baseline.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Offline vs online evals; task-specific metrics; regression thresholds; critical failures.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 37 \'97 Agent Observability\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Trace agent, LLM, retriever, tools and final response as one business workflow.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Track model, tool, latency, tokens, errors, retries, loops, cost and eval result.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Instrument the complete capstone with OpenTelemetry.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Demonstrate one failed request and identify the responsible component from its trace.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 AI observability vs AI evaluation; cross-agent correlation; span attribution; safe logging.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs42\fsmilli21333 \cf2 PART XVIII \'97 Architecture and System Design\

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 38 \'97 Java System Design\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Scalability, availability, reliability and consistency.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Load balancing, caching, queues, replication, partitioning, sharding, CDN and rate limiting.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Distributed locks, idempotency and backpressure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Design URL shortener, notification system, payment service, order platform, document search, chat service and agentic workflow platform.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 For each: requirements \uc0\u8594  API \u8594  data model \u8594  architecture \u8594  scaling \u8594  failures \u8594  security \u8594  observability \u8594  trade-offs.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Practice 30\'9645 minute system-design interviews and defend architectural choices.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs42\fsmilli21333 \cf2 PART XIX \'97 Interview Preparation\

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 39 \'97 Java Interview Track\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Java language, collections, generics, streams, JVM and concurrency.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Spring, Boot, JPA, SQL, REST, security, testing, Kafka, microservices, Docker, AWS and system design.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Prepare each topic at three explanation depths: 30 seconds, 2 minutes, and 5 minutes plus implementation example.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Use implementation evidence rather than memorized definitions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 40 \'97 Coding Interview Track\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Arrays/strings, maps/sets, two pointers, sliding window, stacks/queues, linked lists, binary search, trees, graphs, heaps, intervals, backtracking and dynamic programming.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 For every problem: clarify \uc0\u8594  brute force \u8594  pattern \u8594  optimize \u8594  implement \u8594  test \u8594  time complexity \u8594  space complexity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Explain strategy and Big-O before and after implementation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 41 \'97 Spring Coding Interviews\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Timed API implementation and incremental feature addition.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 60 min: Customer CRUD + PostgreSQL + validation + exceptions + tests.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 90 min: add JWT + pagination + filtering.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 120 min: add Kafka + Redis + Testcontainers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Practice narrating design choices while coding.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 42 \'97 AI/FDE Interviews\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Agent vs workflow; deterministic boundaries; authorization and tool safety.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 RAG evaluation; retrieval failure; loop control; idempotency; state; tracing; safe deployment.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Conduct architecture defense sessions using the capstone.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Answer scenario questions with requirement clarification, design choice, implementation, evaluation and operational controls.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Interview Preparation\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Why agent instead of workflow? When should an LLM not decide? How do you prevent unauthorized tools? How do you evaluate RAG? How do you trace multi-agent workflows?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs42\fsmilli21333 \cf2 PART XX \'97 Final Capstone\

\fs37\fsmilli18667 \cf3 Unit 43 \'97 Production Agentic Enterprise Application\

\fs32 Theory\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Integrate Java/Spring, distributed systems, security, AI, agentic workflows and observability into one defensible architecture.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs32 \cf3 Practice\
\pard\pardeftab720\li422\fi-231\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 \'95 
\f5\b0 Implement Spring Boot REST APIs, PostgreSQL/JPA, Redis, Kafka, Security/OAuth/JWT, Docker, Testcontainers, CI/CD and AWS.\

\f4\b \'95 
\f5\b0 Add Spring AI, embeddings, pgvector, RAG/citations, structured LLM output, tools, single-agent and multi-step workflows.\

\f4\b \'95 
